Subject: server: build the api-key-exempt endpoint set from --api-prefix
Upstream issue: https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/22474
                "server: --api-key doesn't recognize public endpoints when
                --api-prefix is set" — opened 2026-04-28, closed 2026-06-12 as
                `not_planned` by a STALE BOT with no maintainer response. Never
                triaged, and STILL PRESENT ON MASTER (verified 2026-09-01:
                tools/server/server-http.cpp:197 is still a `static const` set of
                BARE literals, and :213 still exact-matches `req.path`).
Applies to:     TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ 337f08e82a861194fd5fc93121205c7c6bc81ddf
                (tools/server/server-http.cpp)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
`--api-prefix` and `--api-key` are each fine alone and BROKEN TOGETHER.

  * Every route is registered as `path_prefix + path` — see
    server_http_context::get()/::post() (:526, :545) and the Web UI block (:376-395),
    where `path_prefix = params.api_prefix` (:80). With `--api-prefix /llama` the
    health route lives at `/llama/health`.
  * The api-key-exempt set (:177) is built from BARE literals — "/health",
    "/v1/health", "/models", "/v1/models", "/", plus each embedded UI asset at
    "/" + name — and the middleware (:198) tests
    `get_public_endpoints.count(req.path)`: an EXACT match against the RAW WIRE PATH.
  * That middleware is installed with `set_pre_routing_handler` (:274), so it runs
    BEFORE routing. Nothing has stripped the prefix by then, and nothing will.

⇒ With both flags set, `/llama/health` is not in the set and returns 401. That is not
merely cosmetic here: droste's healthcheck reads 401 as unhealthy, podman restarts the
container, and the box enters a restart loop that ejects every interactive shell. It
also 401s `/llama/` and `/llama/index.html`, i.e. the user's Web UI is unusable.

WHY WE CARRY IT
===============
Jei's ruling: "if someone turns on the api-prefix, I don't want it to fail purely based
on that." Both settings are exposed on llama's config surface, so the pair is reachable
by a user who did nothing wrong.

🚨 THIS PATCH IS PERMANENT, NOT A STOPGAP. Upstream's own rules do not permit us to
re-file #22474 and we are not pursuing it by any other route ("Someone else might refile,
but not us."). NOBODY IS WATCHING FOR THIS TO BECOME UNNECESSARY. Treat it as load-bearing
indefinitely and expect it to survive every pin bump.

NOT A WIDENING. With a key set and no prefix, `GET /health` already returns 200 — these
endpoints are public by upstream's design. Prefixing restores parity with the no-prefix
case; it makes nothing public that was not already public. The LIST is untouched: same
five paths and the same UI assets, only relocated to where they are actually served.

`params.api_prefix` defaults to "" (common/common.h:608), so `path_prefix + "/health"`
is byte-identical to "/health" on an unprefixed server. Fully backward compatible.

WHAT WOULD MAKE IT REMOVABLE (a condition to NOTICE, not an outcome to expect)
==============================================================================
If a future pin ever builds this set from the configured prefix itself, this patch stops
applying and the anchor guard in scaffolding/Container.llama-build goes red. THAT GUARD IS
THE ONLY THING THAT WILL EVER TELL US. When it fires, read the new source before assuming
anything: it may mean upstream fixed it (drop this file), or merely that the fork moved
and the hunks need re-deriving at the new SHA.

⚠️ DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS FROM ggml-org MASTER. Master has since DROPPED "/models",
"/v1/models" and "/" from the set and replaced the asset loop with a `frontend_paths` set.
Copying master's list would silently change WHICH endpoints are public on our pin. Derive
from the pinned fork's own source.

WHY THESE MECHANISMS
====================
* `static` REMOVED. A function-local static initialises once per PROCESS, so it would
  freeze whichever prefix it saw first — the value would still not track its input, which
  is the same class of bug. server_http_context is a per-instance type; the set belongs to
  the instance. Cost is one small set built at init.
* Captured BY VALUE into `middleware_validate_api_key`. That lambda is stored in the
  httplib server via set_pre_routing_handler and outlives init()'s stack frame, so a
  reference capture of the (now non-static) local would dangle. `[api_keys = params.api_keys]`
  right beside it is the same by-value init-capture idiom.
* `[this]` on the builder lambda to reach the `path_prefix` member, matching
  `middleware_server_state = [this](...)` two blocks down.

TWO HUNKS, AND NEITHER USES A BLANK CONTEXT LINE
================================================
A canonical unified diff spells a blank context line as a single space, and our editing
tools strip trailing whitespace — an empty context line makes GNU patch 2.8 apply this
with fuzz 1 and fail outright under -F0, which is exactly the strictness the anchor guard
wants. Splitting at the blank line between `}();` and the middleware avoids the question.
The second hunk therefore has trailing context only; its deleted line is unique in the
file, so it still anchors exactly.

